Similar to the controversial status of first-person reports 
vis-a-vis theoretical confirmability/falsifiability, the 
linguistics community is divided over the value of 
analyses based on subjective assessments of acceptability.  
It is, of course, common for linguists of many persuasions 
to construct ad-hoc sentences/utterances in service 
of theoretical claims.  Such methods 
raise two questions: whether samples not taken from 
real-life corpora are empirically valid, and whether a 
language-user can simply take their own judgment 
(rather than conducting some survey) as determintive, 
as to whether a sentence is meaningful or grammatical.  

There are many analogies, I find, between the epistemic 
relevance of subjective accounts in linguistics 
and in phenomenology.  Normal empirical disputation 
against publically-accessible data perhaps does not 
apply; but, I'd content, human beings are sufficiently 
able to discuss first-personal states in a collective 
setting that we can compare our experiences and 
analyses.  The possibility of one writer assenting 
or objecting to another's formulation implies that 
claims largely endorsed by a discource community 
serves witness to their theoretical value %-- no 
less than claims warranted by more empirical measures.

In one discussion that elucidates metascientific 
background for these issues, Hubert Kowelewski 
presents Cognitive Grammar in terms of "/Structural Empiricism/":

Viewed through the lens of structural empiricism, Cognitive Grammar
is therefore a family of models of a linguistic system. Like any other scientific theory, it features theoretical entities and processes (cognitive domains,
constructional schemas, compositional paths, subjectification, etc.) and empirical substructures isomorphic (in a weaker version: similar) to appearances
of observable phenomena. (p. 7)

He immediately avows "c/It is somewhat unclear what should count as observable phenomena in 
linguistics/", but situations such uncertainty in a larger philosophical context.  
In particular, */most*/ scientific theories entertain taxonomies and analyses of 
a domain of entities encompassing both public observables and "/hypothesized/" 
posits that %-- for one of several possible reasons %-- cannot be "/seen/".  
Empiricism is */structural/* insofar as such "t/unobservables/" can be 
taken seriously because they constribute to persuasive explanations 
of */observables'/* behavior, with structural models unifying the seen 
and the hidden into a rational package.  Sometimes the unseen posits are 
deemed to be just as real as everything else, merely beyond our capabilities 
of (instrument-enhanced) observation, at least for now.  Other phenomena 
(like "t/dark energy/") are left more open-ended, possibly subsumed under 
such ontological realism or possibly artifacts of some more complex process 
(like "t/phonons/", which behave like "i/particles/" of sound, but 
that's largely a metaphor).  

The status of mental attidues and subjectivity vis-a-vis physics and material 
science is more circuitous than these other more-physical "t/unobservables/", 
but Kowaleski implies that we should treat the cognitive as adjunct to the 
physical in a similar structural vein: if explanations quantifying 
over subjective states provide convincing accounts of observed 
behavior (in this case, linguistic performance as a whole), then the first-personal 
realm should be judged scientifically legitimate as a kind of "/hidden variables/" 
dimension.  Specifically in regard to ad-hoc sentence examples, he argues as follows:

The linguistic material analyzed in many of Langacker’s texts appears to be
either constructed or at least picked in order to illustrate points under analysis. For this reason, the analyses can be easily attacked on the grounds that
they are somehow unreliable due to the inauthenticity of data. ... 
Many linguists seem to embrace the conviction that linguistics should
study only actual, real-life events of language use, which do not belong anywhere on the above 
counterfactuality scale [viz., logical impossibility is much stronger 
than the contingent fact of something not having yet occurred, with 
various intermediates].  However, this conviction overlooks the fact 
that many useful scientific models involve clearly counterfactual scenarios 
[whose] role is not merely heuristic or pedagogical. [para] 
The use of fictional models in natural sciences can be accounted for in
philosophical terms. In the framework of structural empiricism, isolated
systems, frictionless pendulums, and perfect liquids are treated just like
any other theoretical objects, except their theoreticity does not follow from
unobservability, as is the case with electrons and electromagnetic fields, but
from their fictional character.  Since a structural empiricist is not 
committed to believing in the actual existence of real-world objects corresponding
to theoretical concepts, but only to the empirical adequacy of the theory
featuring these concepts, the structural empiricist may accept the theory
while withholding belief in the actual real-world existence of the objects
postulated by the theory.  An idealized model may serve as a tool for predicting observable 
phenomena when predictions derived from the model are
adjusted with respect to the factors omitted in the idealization.

A reasonable paraphrase vis-a-vis constructed sentences is that, for purposes 
of expositing semantic or syntactic claims, the universe of linguistic 
objects is broader than merely the totality of sentences humans have in 
fact created.  Ad-hoc hypothetical sentences also belong to the 
linguistic domain if we consider this domain to include materials 
for idealized model-building.

I personally would put it thus: what makes a sentence an object in the 
domain of entities spotlighted by linguistics is not necessarily the 
fact of its being empirically an example of real language-use, but 
rather because upon that sentence we can apply observables, analyses, 
annotations, and so forth whose ordinary ground is real-world 
sentences %-- we can note syntactic, morphological, or lexical patterns, 
and so forth.  By analogy, complex numbers are not mathematical objects 
because they have real-world interpretation (as spatial distance, say), but 
because we can apply quantitative techniques to them (addition, multiplication, 
exponentiation, differentiation and integration, etc.).     

My earlier subway-platform speculation was similarly about hypothetical 
circumstances.  I caught New York subways many times in real life, 
but that's beside the point: we are primed to learn from experience, 
but in a manner that abstracts nonsalient details.  So vividly memorializing 
past experience is a deviation from ordinary rationality.  With respect 
to the intellectual processes involved in learning from experience, 
distilling pattern from */hypothetical/* scenarios is arguably 
more realistic than introspective reconstructions of real ones.  
Husserl, too, builds many analyses around constructed situations %-- 
a mannequin in a shop window, our perceptual synthesis of a sphere 
%-- and likewise almost all of his commentators.

More to the point, my presentation was not free-associative: via posits 
like attention, episodicity, and situationality I bore a matrix of 
concepts via which to, in effect, "/annotate/" elements of consciousness.  
Instead of focusing on the contrast between real and hypothetical 
experiences, methodology should consider whether some target of 
analysis is amenable to the notions and structures comprising an 
analytic protocol.  Such framing is probably not structural */empiricism/* 
a la Kowalewski, but there is a similar emphasis on structural 
model building.  A hypothetical object, space, or situation becomes 
an instance of a theoretical model via structural adequacy, and might 
clarify structural hypotheses, or their rationale, better than 
real-world examples.  By analogy, again, complex numbers %-- which 
lack physical interpretations %-- are 
better models than reals, in some respects, for a certain kind of 
mathematical reasoning (e.g., every polynomial has complex roots).

It seems to me that the status of constructed examples in linguistics 
and phenomenology is similar, and the "t/demarcation problem/" 
%-- defending linguistics as a legitimate science, not pseudoscience 
(astrology, say) %-- plays similarly in both contexts.  There is 
a more acute version of that problem once we accept hypothetical 
case-studies as a foundation of argumentation; scientists might 
accept corpus analysis, or the measurable 
performance of AI agents designed according to computational linguistics principles, 
or (respectively) quantitative experimental psychology, but dismiss 
aritificial sentences or "c/just so/" phenomenology as quackery.  
"r/Structural model building/" as an apologia, I'd say, works similarly 
too: via Cognitive Grammar we have a repertoire of concepts 
(landmark, projector, profiling, speaker epistemics) modeled 
within constructed sentences no less than real ones.

Suppose I want to examine a pair of hypothetical examples like:

(1)  John poured water onto the stove.
(2)  John spilled water onto the stove.

The most obvious point would be how "poured" versus "spilled" frame the 
action in different ways (deliberate or accidental).  By selecting 
one or the other, the speaker implictly expresses beliefs about 
John's mental state.  So, choice of words can encode 
information about the speaker's epistemic starting-point; the 
sentence is not merely notating observable details.  

But let's step back.  For sake of discussion, I will assume (1) and (2) 
occur contexts where listeners know the speaker saw John firsthand 
(the evidence is direct observation, not hearsay).  Implicit 
in a communicative claim to */describe/* something witnessed 
is having been in a perceptual orientation to */see/* it.  The 
sentences then encode salient features of this orientation: 
presumably the speaker was standing/sitting in a position 
where both John and the stove were in their visual frame, 
and they could focus attention on one or the other as 
called for.  Likewise, probably they were talking to 
John or in some other way clued in to the ambient situation, so 
as to rationally infer John's intentions.  Much of the 
*/configuration/* of this cognitive-perceptual orientation, 
thereby summarized, reflects my sketched "t/structural model/" 
for phenomenological description: the intentional relations toward 
objects insofar as we deliberately orient toward them 
as a focus of attention; the coexistence of multiple 
attentional sites (e.g., John and the stove) with focus 
shifting back and forth in larger synthesizing episodes; 
the role of larger-scale situational awareness guiding 
our granular decisions to direct attention hither and thither.

And yet, such configurations are also implicit in the 
sentences' meanings.  Foci of attention tend to become 
signified as nouns; so in both cases we have "John" and 
"the stove".  It is John who initiates the action that 
affects the stove's state, so the former becomes encoded 
as subject and the latter as object.  Since speakers 
implicitly expresses the evidence for propositions 
stated (or implied) in their discourse, understanding a 
sentence is partly a matter of inferring mental states, 
which includes perceptual comportment toward 
its noun-topics.  Anyone hearing (1) and (2) will, 
at least in some schematic manner, reconstruct a 
configuration of attentional focus and situational 
construal that I tried to explicate "h/phenomenologically/".
Syntactic categories like nouns and verbs are 
rooted in listeners' need to infer speaker's cognitive states.

Notice that communication leaves some intersubjective 
slack: our reconstruction of fellows' mental 
world is only provisional.  As a feature of our 
reason and lifeworld, that is, mental states exist 
in a vivid way in our own momentary consciousness, 
but they also exist in an approximative manner 
vis-a-vis people (and often animals) around us.  
Others' cognitions being, modulo such imprecision, 
a natural feature of our own rationality undergirds 
%-- so I claim %-- the feasibility of both 
phenomenological and linguistic analysis.

Listeners inferring speakers' unstated beliefs, 
and perceptual orientations, does not involve 
extrasensory powers but rather deployment of a 
strutural model for how people comport themselves 
to situations so as to observe and act rationally.  
Because language-use is a crucial factor in our 
purposeful action, we seamlessly transition 
from nonverbal orientation toward surrounding 
environments to framings of states of affairs 
in communicable ways, then actual speech-production.  
Basic organization principles migrate from one to 
the other, such that cognitive-perceptual structures 
(attentional foci, say) become manifest, 
within communicative intent and staging, as 
linguistic phenomena (e.g., that to which 
I can perceptually attend becomes a noun).

If this is correct, then posits of linguistic argumentation 
have origins in cognitive faculties that can be 
phenomenologically investigated (and should be if we 
want an encompassing, "/in vivo/" theory).  At the 
same time, language elucidates  
how models of other minds are inherent to our 
rational agency; they aren't esoteric superstitions 
of a phenomenological sect.  Language is the 
best case-study for how phenomenology simply 
codifies "t/folk theories/" of mind and cognition.  

Phenomenology and cognitive linguistics, then, to 
my mind reinforce each other and each can be said 
to depend on the other, from an expository 
and justificatory point of view.  



(Contrast my defense with Kowalewski's study of the 
Langacker sentence(s) "//").


